meta {
  name: TRON USDT-TRC20 deposit (via fake TronGrid)
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

post {
  url: {{trongrid_sim_base}}/_sim/transfer
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "to": "REPLACE_WITH_DEPOSIT_ADDRESS",
    "amount6": "9990000",
    "confirmations": 20
  }
}

docs {
  Simulates a fully-confirmed TRC-20 USDT deposit.

  TRON has NO push webhooks — the worker POLLS TronGrid — so this deliberately
  does not post to the app. It queues a transfer inside
  `scripts/fake-trongrid.mjs`, a local stand-in for api.trongrid.io, and the
  REAL apps/worker polling path then discovers it. That exercises amount-tag
  matching against the single receive wallet, confirmation counting,
  settlement and delivery for real, instead of pretending an ingestion
  endpoint exists.

  Setup:
  1. `node scripts/fake-trongrid.mjs`            # listens on :8099
  2. `TRONGRID_API_BASE=http://localhost:8099 pnpm --filter @tgshop/worker dev`
  3. Set `to` above to your TRON_RECEIVE_ADDRESS, create a TRON order in the
     Mini App and copy the EXACT tagged amount it shows (e.g. 29.0057 USDT ->
     `amount6` 29005700): the sub-cent tag is what identifies the invoice.

  `amount6` is USDT in 6-decimal integer units — 9990000 = 9.99 USDT, never a
  float. `confirmations` is how deep the transfer is mined: set it below
  TRON_MIN_CONFIRMATIONS (default 19) to watch the worker correctly refuse to
  settle, then `POST {{trongrid_sim_base}}/_sim/advance {"blocks": 20}` to
  bury it deeper and see it settle.
}
